# Lesson 28 — Expected Value, Type I/II Errors, and Conditional Probability

## Expected Value
Expected value is the probability-weighted average outcome across possible results.

```text
EV = Σ [P(outcome) × value(outcome)]
```

It is useful for comparing decisions under uncertainty, such as expected revenue, expected loss, or expected experiment impact.

## Type I and Type II Errors
- **Type I error:** Rejecting a true null hypothesis. A false positive.
- **Type II error:** Failing to reject a false null hypothesis. A false negative.

Statistical power is the probability of detecting a real effect when one exists.

## Conditional Probability
```text
P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B)
```

The denominator is the population for which condition B is already known to be true.

## Interview Takeaway
Translate probability questions into populations and outcomes before calculating. For hypothesis testing, explain the real business consequence of false positives and false negatives rather than only defining the terms.
